  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nA weak and moisture-starved upper level trough will cross the \nPacific Northwest on Saturday, pushing a dry cold front through \nthe area. While precipitation chances remain near zero due to \nlimited mid and low level moisture, the frontal passage will \neffectively break down the upper level ridge and kick off a \ncooling trend across the region. Surface winds will shift west \nto northwest and become breezy at times on Saturday behind the \nfront, especially across the Magic Valley and higher terrain.\n\nBy Sunday, the next upper level trough approaches the West\nCoast. This will back upper level flow across the region to the\nsouth and southwest, maintaining dry conditions and near-normal\ntemperatures through early next week. Latest ensemble and\ndeterministic guidance now favors the trough splitting along the\nWest Coast and eventually forming a closed low off the southern\nCalifornia coast Tuesday/Wednesday. This path keeps the primary\nthreat of precipitation focused west of the Cascades and mostly\noutside our forecast area through Wednesday. There is a limited\nchance for precipitation (15-20%) across south-central Idaho if\nenough moisture can be advected northward Wednesday. Expect\nafternoon highs to drop back into the 60s for most valley\nlocations starting Monday and continuing through midweek.",
